WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:07.650
Some of the programs you'll use on the mainframe are also known as utilities.

2
00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:14.850
Utilities perform a specific task and can be called through JCL to do things like copy data sets and members,

3
00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:22.930
sort records, generate data. In this section, we'll take a look at just a few of the more commonly used ZOS utilities.

4
00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:26.770
We'll start with system utilities. Here you'll find

5
00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.950
ICKDSF, which is used to install,

6
00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:36.110
initialize, and manage DASD. There's also IDH init with two T's at the end.

7
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.630
That's used to initialize tapes for long-term storage.

8
00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:45.670
And there's IEH list, which is a utility used to list entries in a PDS

9
00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:53.190
to look at a DASD's volume table of contents or VTOC or to view an operating system volumes catalog.

10
00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:57.230
You'll notice that with these there's a sort of a format going on.

11
00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:02.430
There's a prefix followed by an abbreviated representation of what it does.

12
00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:08.590
Well, the prefixes correspond with the ZOS component that they work in. For example,

13
00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:19.070
ICK is device support facilities, IEH is data facility product, DFP, and IEF is allocation,

14
00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:24.910
IPL, JES, scheduler, and well, it's actually a number of core components.

15
00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:29.630
Then there's data set utilities, which typically start with IEB.

16
00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:36.630
You may be wondering why we're spending so much time on data sets and why there are so many data set utilities.

17
00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.190
Well, the simple fact is that the mainframe deals in data.

18
00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:47.510
It's constantly creating records based on what applications are doing and those applications are updating records.

19
00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:51.750
And there's always going to be need to take information created in one place and

20
00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:56.270
repurpose it to be used as input for a programmer made into a report.

21
00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:00.710
More importantly, it means you can handle those types of situations quickly

22
00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:08.430
instead of editing records individually line by line by hand because you needed to remove the fifth column of each record.

23
00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:14.630
Another word about utilities, a lot of times the utility is multi-purpose like a Swiss Army knife.

24
00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:20.910
So if you use one set of parameters, it'll create a data set but use it with another set of parameters

25
00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.110
and it generates debug data.

26
00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:28.830
And the only way to really know what a utility is capable of is to look at the documentation.

27
00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:33.630
I'll also point out that you can often use different utilities to do the same thing.

28
00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:38.830
They might do them slightly differently, but there's more than one way to copy a data set.

29
00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.110
So let's look at that IEB copy.

30
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:48.190
As you might guess, it's there for copying, but it can also do merges of PDS's and PDSE's.

31
00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:56.190
It can do compression of PDS's and PDSE's and it can even convert PDS's into PDSE's and vice versa.

32
00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.830
Here's what invoking it looks like.

33
00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:02.710
We're using it here to copy one PDS into another PDS.

34
00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:07.230
There's no input needed, just specifying the two data sets right down here.

35
00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.750
Quick quiz on IEB copy and it's a true or false.

36
00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:14.230
True or false.

37
00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:18.510
I can use IEB copy to merge VSAM data sets.

38
00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.270
The answer to that is false.

39
00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.310
It's for PDS's and PDSE's only.

40
00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:30.590
When we want to work in VSAM data sets, we need to use another utility, which we'll take a look at later.

41
00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:36.470
Here's IE beginner, which does not mean that it's utility for people brand new to the mainframe.

42
00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:44.750
The name is IEB, the prefix for dataset utility, and then G-E-N-E-R for generate records.

43
00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:51.150
And it can be used to back up a dataset, produce a partition dataset from members of a sequential dataset,

44
00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:57.470
print out the contents of datasets, and even convert data formats and change settings for a dataset.

45
00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.510
Very nice to know about all those capabilities.

46
00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:03.430
Here it is getting called through JCL.

47
00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:11.670
We're copying one dataset to another, and you can see we're also specifying a few parameters about how we want that new dataset to be created.

48
00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:18.910
DF sort is a high performance sort, merge, copy, analysis, and reporting product for ZOS,

49
00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:22.230
and it can handle data at the record, field, and bit level.

50
00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:28.310
This is one of the biggest Swiss Army knives out there because it can sort, merge, and copy.

51
00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:32.190
But while it's doing that, it can also reformat data along the way.

52
00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:37.390
So you can use it to change data formats, edit, delete, and add fields, repeat records.

53
00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:43.830
You can even use it to perform arithmetic on data, summing up values and records while you're sorting or merging them.

54
00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:52.830
And it's important to know about all the capabilities of these utilities because you will be in a situation where you're presented with a set of data that looks one way,

55
00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:56.390
and it needs to be formatted in a different way for a program to take it.

56
00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:04.950
When you're able to say, I finally got to use that function of DF sort or IAB copy, and it works, it's a great feeling.

57
00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:13.310
So before writing your own code to do something and starting from scratch, check the utilities because chances are someone else had to do it before,

58
00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.870
and maybe there's a utility for it you can use.

59
00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:20.910
Lastly, I want to show you a special program, IEF-BR14.

60
00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:24.470
This is an IEF, so it has to do with allocation.

61
00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:30.950
And I can tell you that BR14 means branch to register 14, which is an assembler instruction.

62
00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:37.310
But all you really need to know is that IEF-BR14 is a program designed to do nothing.

63
00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:48.830
So you remember how in JCL you can specify a new catalog delete for a data set and it will allocate a new member and it will catalog that member if the job ran correctly?

64
00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:52.430
Well, what if all we want to do is create a new data set?

65
00:05:53.110 --> 00:06:02.830
That method in JCL is a pretty handy way of doing it, and it's a shame we have to do it while calling a program because I'm really not interested in what running that program is actually going to do.

66
00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:06.030
That's where IEF-BR14 comes in.

67
00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:14.750
It lets you use those JCL features while executing a program, and that program is actually just there because you have to specify a program.

68
00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:15.590
It does nothing.

69
00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:24.350
So in this example, we're creating a new data set with new catalog, and we're not specifying the third parameter because we just know it's going to work.

70
00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:28.790
And then we're also using this DD statement to delete an existing data set.

71
00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:34.150
It's kind of a hack, but it's an official hack and people use it all over the place.

72
00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:38.830
Actually know a guy who has IEF-BR14 as their custom license plate.

73
00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:41.270
Last one, IDCAMS.

74
00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:46.270
This one is for generating and modifying VSAM as well as non-VSAM data sets.

75
00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:53.470
And I feel like I keep saying this, but you become an expert in IDCAMS and you can get that across to an employer.

76
00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:55.550
That's time well spent right there.

77
00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:59.670
Speaking of time well spent, that brings us to the end of this lesson.
